>> "ri'e", yes. Not so sure about "zo'o" though. It's usually used as
>> standalone, and your grammar doesn't allow ROhA to stand by itself.
>>
>
> Yes, {zo'o} can't stand for itself. It is described as a modifier of
> interjections so I ban it here.
> When people use {zo'o} alone then according to altatufa ideology it
> implies that interjection core was elided. altatufa
> autorestores {ju'a} in such cases leading to {ju'a zo'o}.
>

I don't know, what about things that are not assertions:

-  zo'o do se nazbi xo da

-  zo'o ko citka ta

I think "zo'o" is more like "le'o", "vu'e", "ga'i", a stance, a mental or
emotional position adopted by the speaker. What are the arguments to make
it an interjection modifier?

This can be tested using the current stable version of altatufa:
> http://mw.lojban.org/extensions/ilmentufa/altatufa-stodi.html
>

It doesn't seem to work for me.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

